{"attack": true, "case": "The claim is self-undermining: it asserts the preference for better-corroborated theories is 'non-inductive' while simultaneously requiring 'a pragmatic, non-justificatory faith in the uniformity of nature.' Faith in the uniformity of nature is precisely the inductive principle Hume identified — the assumption that the future will resemble the past. Relabeling this faith as 'pragmatic' and 'non-justificatory' does not change its logical character; it merely disguises the inductive step as a convention. The target's own countercondition #3 captures this exact objection ('the practical outcome is identical to inductivism') yet the claim provides no resolution — it merely lists the objection as an 'observation' and proceeds as if labeling the inductive assumption a 'departure' neutralizes it. Furthermore, the mechanism admits the rule involves 'projecting past success into future utility,' which is induction by definition, then claims the circularity is 'resolved' by calling it a 'conventionalist decision.' Calling a logical move conventionalist does not alter its inferential structure; if the rule's utility depends on past corroboration predicting future test-suitability, it is inductive regardless of the pragmatic framing.", "counterexample": ["Consider two agents: Agent A (inductivist) prefers a theory because past survival makes it more probable in the future. Agent B (the target's 'non-inductive' agent) prefers a theory because past corroboration makes it better suited for future critical testing. Both require the assumption that past testing performance is relevant to future testing performance — uniformity of nature. Agent B labels this assumption 'pragmatic faith'; Agent A labels it 'induction.' The inferential structure is identical: past performance → future expectation. The label difference does not make B's rule non-inductive."], "premise": "A methodological rule whose practical utility depends on assuming that past test performance is informative about future test performance is inductive, regardless of whether that assumption is labeled 'pragmatic faith' rather than 'probability.'", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "73fa56607709fbe102a21", "quote": "Preferring the better-corroborated theory is defensible on Popper's terms not as an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past, but as a non-inductive methodological rule to maximize the severity of future tests (truth-likeness potential), provided one accepts the 'departure' that this preference relies on a pragmatic, non-justificatory faith in the uniformity of nature"}, {"block": "73fa56607709fbe102a21", "quote": "The apparent circularity of projecting past success into future utility is resolved by framing it as a conventionalist decision to play the game of science optimally, while admitting that acting on any theory outside the laboratory requires a metaphysical faith that Popper conceded was unavoidable for living beings but irrelevant to the objective validity of the scientific method itself."}], "successor_question": "Can a rule whose stated utility requires projecting past corroboration into future test-suitability be meaningfully distinguished from induction merely by labeling the required uniformity assumption 'pragmatic faith' rather than 'probabilistic inference'?"}